  "backgroundImageAltText": "Four-panel diagram of hepatocyte compartments in ethanol metabolism: cytosol, smooth endoplasmic reticulum, peroxisome and mitochondrion",
  "hotspots": [
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 25,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "header": "Cytosol — Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH)",
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>ADH is the main pathway at ordinary intakes. It oxidises ethanol to acetaldehyde and reduces NAD⁺ to NADH. This is the first NADH-generating step of alcohol metabolism.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "bc18ddf5-7323-4bf1-be85-aef1d522f379",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Cytosol — Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH)",
            "authors": [],
            "changes": [],
            "extraTitle": ""
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 25,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "header": "Smooth ER — Microsomal Ethanol-Oxidising System (MEOS)",
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>CYP2E1 uses NADPH and O₂ to oxidise ethanol to acetaldehyde. It has lower affinity for ethanol and becomes important only at high blood alcohol levels. Chronic drinking induces CYP2E1, increasing ethanol clearance but also generating reactive oxygen species.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "197a048d-8ed2-43ed-9f1a-fd85263e1094",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Smooth ER — Microsomal Ethanol-Oxidising System (MEOS)",
            "authors": [],
            "changes": [],
            "extraTitle": ""
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 75,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "header": "Peroxisomes — Catalase",
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>Catalase uses H₂O₂ to oxidise ethanol to acetaldehyde. It plays a quantitatively minor role in overall ethanol metabolism.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "e02f9345-e71c-48eb-b332-89054cbe59ab",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Peroxisomes — Catalase",
            "authors": [],
            "changes": [],
            "extraTitle": ""
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 75,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "header": "Mitochondria — Aldehyde Dehydrogenase (ALDH2)",
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>ALDH2 oxidises acetaldehyde to acetate and generates NADH. When acetaldehyde was formed by ADH, this is the second NADH generated in the ethanol-to-acetate pathway. Individuals with the inactive ALDH2*2 variant accumulate acetaldehyde and experience the flush reaction.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "c6a1f1b5-ed69-45ef-b83d-92f225fdd60d",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Mitochondria — Aldehyde Dehydrogenase (ALDH2)",
            "authors": [],
            "changes": [],
            "extraTitle": ""
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
